==============================================================================
2. Bundled FFmpeg sidecar (and related libraries) — GPL notice
==============================================================================

LS.Video may be distributed together with a static Windows `ffmpeg.exe`
sidecar (Tauri `externalBin`, typically under `src-tauri/binaries/`). That
binary is a separate helper process used for proxy generation, trimming, and
related media work. It is not part of the MIT-licensed application source.

FFmpeg is free software licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License
(LGPL) version 2.1 or later, or the GNU General Public License (GPL) version 2
or later, depending on how it is configured and which libraries it is linked
against. See:

  https://ffmpeg.org/legal.html
  https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg

A build that links **libx264** (or other GPL-only components) is **GPL**.
Typical full-featured Windows sidecars used with LS.Video fall into this
category. libx264 is available from VideoLAN:

  https://www.videolan.org/developers/x264.html

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compliance when shipping the sidecar with LS.Video
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When you ship `ffmpeg.exe` (or an equivalent FFmpeg binary) next to LS.Video:

1. Keep the app’s own license clear for *your* code (section 1 — MIT for
   LS.Video application source).

2. State that the bundled FFmpeg binary is GPL (when built with GPL components
   such as libx264) and that corresponding source for that binary is available.
   Source means the FFmpeg and (if applicable) x264 upstream trees/tags you
   built from, or another offer of source that satisfies the GPL obligations
   for the exact binary you distribute.

3. Do **not** claim the whole installer or application bundle is MIT-only if
   it includes this sidecar. The distribution is a combination of MIT
   application code and GPL (or LGPL, for LGPL-only builds) third-party
   binaries.

4. This project only produces or ships a helper binary; it does **not**
   relicense FFmpeg or x264. Upstream copyrights and license terms remain with
   their respective authors.

Corresponding source for common upstream trees can be obtained from the
project sites above. If you build or repackage a custom FFmpeg binary, you
must make source for *that* build available under the same GPL/LGPL terms that
apply to it.

Nothing in this file grants you rights beyond those already granted by the
applicable MIT, LGPL, or GPL licenses for each component.
